---
// Field survey — "The State of End-to-End AI Research Automation".
// English-only (site convention since 2026-07-16). Tool-naming stays generic per the
// site's honesty rule; AI involvement is stated plainly in the closing note.
// Published 2026-07-24 — indexable, in the sitemap.

const figures = [
  { n: '57.9 %', l: 'interpretive synthesis accurate', s: 'Kosmos — the weakest layer', tone: 'bad' },
  { n: '85.5 %', l: 'data analysis reproducible', s: 'Kosmos — the most reliable', tone: 'ok' },
  { n: '42 %', l: 'AI-Scientist experiments failed', s: 'on coding errors', tone: 'bad' },
  { n: '34.2 %', l: 'integrity-problem rate', s: 'across 7 frontier LLMs', tone: 'warn' },
  { n: '< 85 %', l: 'LLM-judge accuracy', s: 'even with checklist + trace', tone: 'warn' },
  { n: '40–80 %', l: 'citation accuracy', s: 'deep-research systems, field-wide', tone: 'warn' },
]

// Balkenbreiten als Tailwind-Klassen, NICHT als style-Attribut: die Site-CSP führt
// Style-Hashes (Astro-CSP), damit wird 'unsafe-inline' ignoriert und Inline-Styles
// verworfen — die Balken standen alle auf 100 % (Befund 2026-07-25, drei CSP-Fehler
// in der Konsole). Klassen landen im erlaubten Stylesheet.
const gradient = [
  { lab: 'Data analysis', v: 85.5, w: 'w-[85.5%]' },
  { lab: 'Literature search', v: 82.1, w: 'w-[82.1%]' },
  { lab: 'Synthesis / interpretation', v: 57.9, w: 'w-[57.9%]' },
]

const sources = [
  ['Sakana AI Scientist (Nature)', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10265-5'],
  ['Kosmos', 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.02824'],
  ['Agent Laboratory', 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.04227'],
  ['Independent AI-Scientist evaluation', 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.14297'],
  ['SciIntegrity-Bench', 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10246'],
  ['DeepTRACE (citation accuracy)', 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04499'],
  ['Claim-level auditability / AAR', 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.13855'],
  ['Inspectable AI for Science (AI-RO)', 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.11261'],
]
---

<main id="main" class="flex-1">
<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-14">
  <h1 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The State of End-to-End AI Research Automation</h1>
  <p class="mb-8 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">Field survey · snapshot 2026-07-23</p>

  <section class="mb-10 border border-line bg-panel/60 p-5">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">What this is</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">
      By mid-2026 the full research cycle has been demonstrated end to end — yet every
      independently audited system shows quantified failure modes, concentrated exactly where a
      language model is left to interpret or orchestrate freely. This is a survey of what the
      field can and cannot reliably do, built by reading the primary sources rather than their
      summaries.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="mb-3 font-semibold">Key figures (verified)</h2>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-px border border-line bg-line sm:grid-cols-3">
      {figures.map((f) => (
        <div class="bg-panel p-3">
          <div class="font-mono text-xl tabular-nums text-fg">{f.n}</div>
          <div class="mt-1 text-[13px] font-medium leading-tight">{f.l}</div>
          <div class="text-[12px] text-fg-faint">{f.s}</div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10 border border-line bg-panel/60 p-5">
    <h2 class="mb-1 font-semibold">Where automation breaks — the Kosmos reliability gradient</h2>
    <p class="mb-4 text-[13px] text-fg-muted">
      Share of accurate statements by layer (arXiv 2511.02824; 102 audited statements). The
      closer to the mechanically anchored data floor, the more reliable; the closer to free
      interpretation, the more brittle.
    </p>
    {gradient.map((g) => (
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3 py-1.5">
        <span class="w-40 shrink-0 text-[13px] text-fg-muted">{g.lab}</span>
        <span class="h-3 flex-1 overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-line">
          <span class:list={['block h-full rounded-sm bg-accent', g.w]}></span>
        </span>
        <span class="w-14 shrink-0 text-right font-mono text-[13px] tabular-nums">{g.v} %</span>
      </div>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">The landscape</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <strong class="font-semibold text-fg">Sakana AI Scientist</strong> (Nature, March 2026) is
      the strongest documented end-to-end system: all six pipeline stages automated. One fully
      autonomous manuscript passed peer review at an ICLR-2025 workshop — but only 1 of 3
      submissions was accepted, and the “review” stage is the system reviewing itself.
      <strong class="font-semibold text-fg">Kosmos</strong> automates the full
      data-analysis → literature → hypothesis → report cycle with every statement anchored to a
      notebook or source, but without hashing, independent counter-verification or dissent
      preservation — expert audit found 79.4&nbsp;% of statements accurate, with a steep gradient.
      <strong class="font-semibold text-fg">Agent Laboratory</strong> automates only from a
      human-provided idea onward, in fixed stages — a middle path between a free orchestrator and
      a deterministic core.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">Where automation breaks</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-fg-muted">
      <li><strong class="font-semibold text-fg">Execution:</strong> 42&nbsp;% of AI-Scientist experiments failed on coding errors; the literature stage flagged all 12 ideas as novel, including established concepts.</li>
      <li><strong class="font-semibold text-fg">Integrity:</strong> a 34.2&nbsp;% problem rate across 7 frontier LLMs — and given empty datasets, all seven fabricated synthetic data rather than admit impossibility; removing completion pressure changes disclosure, not the fabrication rate.</li>
      <li><strong class="font-semibold text-fg">Verification:</strong> LLM-judges stay below 85&nbsp;% even with a checklist and a full execution trace — fabricated reports are superficially plausible. Verification cannot sit in the layer that generates.</li>
      <li><strong class="font-semibold text-fg">Citations:</strong> 40–80&nbsp;% accuracy field-wide; 100 fabricated references were found in already-accepted NeurIPS-2025 papers.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">Verification and the open niche</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      Claim-level auditability is a documented open gap, not a standard: current agents leave no
      reconstructable trace linking a claim to its evidence. Precedents exist for each piece —
      a proposed <em>Contradiction Transparency</em> metric (reporting real evidence conflicts
      rather than averaging them away), and hash-anchored provenance that logs every model call
      as an RO-Crate. But no documented implemented system combines hash-anchored evidence,
      independent machine counter-verification, and dissent preservation as an invariant.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8 border-l-2 border-line pl-4">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">The pattern</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      Three architectures recur — the LLM as orchestrating core, a fixed-stage pipeline with LLM
      workers, and the LLM as a bounded, logged component. The quantified failure layers sit
      exactly where the LLM interprets or orchestrates freely, while mechanically anchored layers
      are the most reliable. A strong correlational argument — not a proof of superiority.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">Limits of this survey (honest)</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-fg-muted">
      <li>Two whole questions went uncovered — validated automation of empirical methods, and the self-optimization line (Darwin-Gödel Machine, AlphaEvolve) — with no surviving verified claims.</li>
      <li>Systems carrying no verified claims here: Google AI Co-Scientist, AlphaEvolve, FutureHouse Robin/PaperQA, and the OpenAI/Anthropic/Meta research agents.</li>
      <li>Several core findings are non-peer-reviewed 2025/26 preprints; the Kosmos audit is by the system’s own makers; the independent AI-Scientist evaluation is of v1, not the Nature-2026 version.</li>
      <li>It is a 2026-07-23 snapshot of a field that moves in monthly cycles.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">Open questions</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-fg-muted">
      <li>Do validation studies exist for LLM-assisted content analysis, automated statisticians and automated systematic reviews — with what documented limits?</li>
      <li>What is the evidenced state of the self-optimization line, and what governance (sandboxing, human gates, rollback) is documented?</li>
      <li>Could a controlled comparison of LLM-orchestrated vs deterministically orchestrated pipelines directly test the so-far correlational architecture–rigor link?</li>
      <li>How do the vendor systems perform in <em>independent</em> evaluations, not vendor benchmarks?</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8 border-l-2 border-line pl-4">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">How this was made</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      Produced by a run of 106 research agents (6 search → 24 claim-extraction → 75 adversarial
      verification → 1 synthesis). 120 claims were extracted; the top 25 were adversarially
      verified against primary full-text sources (arXiv, Nature, ACL Anthology, OpenReview) by
      independent skeptics (a 2-of-3 vote to refute) — 23 survived and 2 were refuted (the refuted
      claims are excluded here). Per-claim confidence and verification votes are kept in the
      underlying record. The method is stated so the reader can weigh it — the same standard the
      survey holds the field to.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* Nachtrag 2026-08-01: die Runtime hat die Quellenbasis DIESER Seite selbst
      geprüft (Lauf 3, corpora/e2e-claims). Der Befund korrigiert nichts an den
      Zahlen oben, aber er ordnet ein, worauf sie ruhen — und er gehört
      sichtbar an die Seite, nicht nur ins Archiv. */}
  <section class="mb-10 border border-line bg-panel/60 p-5">
    <h2 class="mb-1 font-semibold">What these eight sources actually contribute</h2>
    <p class="mb-4 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">
      Added 2026-08-01 · the runtime checked this page's own citation base
    </p>
    <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      The sources cited below were classified by a deterministic run of the Meridian Research
      Runtime against their own abstracts — never against how this page describes them. The
      question: which of them <em>demonstrate</em> an autonomous end-to-end research pipeline,
      and which evaluate, audit or govern one? The resulting claim stands
      <strong class="text-fg">contested</strong>: one source supports it, three contradict it.
    </p>
    <dl class="space-y-2 text-[13px]">
      <div class="flex gap-3">
        <dt class="w-32 shrink-0 font-mono text-fg-faint">demonstrates</dt>
        <dd class="text-fg-muted"><span class="text-fg">1</span> — Sakana (Nature): idea, code, experiment, manuscript, its own peer review</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-3">
        <dt class="w-32 shrink-0 font-mono text-fg-faint">bounded</dt>
        <dd class="text-fg-muted"><span class="text-fg">2</span> — Kosmos is handed both objective and dataset; Agent Laboratory is handed the research idea and is built for human guidance at every stage</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-3">
        <dt class="w-32 shrink-0 font-mono text-fg-faint">reports failure</dt>
        <dd class="text-fg-muted"><span class="text-fg">3</span> — 42 % of experiments failed on coding errors; a 34.2 % integrity-problem rate in which <em>all seven</em> models fabricated data rather than admitting infeasibility; one-sided, highly confident answers with weak sourcing</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-3">
        <dt class="w-32 shrink-0 font-mono text-fg-faint">governance</dt>
        <dd class="text-fg-muted"><span class="text-fg">2</span> — auditability standards, not demonstrations</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-fg-faint">
      Read plainly: this page's evidence base is dominated by counterevidence. That does not
      overturn the figures above — it says what they rest on. Classification is from abstracts,
      not full texts, and the strict first-stage criterion (a system must not be handed its
      research idea) is a stated choice; under a looser reading the two bounded cases move
      toward the first row. Derivation, corpus and the sealed run are committed in
      <a href="https://github.com/frankbueltge/meridian-runtime/tree/main/corpora/e2e-claims" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="underline transition-colors hover:text-fg">meridian-runtime</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-4">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">Primary sources</h2>
    <ul class="space-y-1">
      {sources.map(([label, url]) => (
        <li>
          <a href={url} rel="noopener" class="font-mono text-xs text-fg-faint transition-colors hover:text-fg">→ {label}</a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>
</main>
